Bundled language grammars
==========================

opencode ships syntect's 75 default grammars plus these extras:

  TOML        — upstream grammar, MIT.
                https://github.com/jasonwilliams/sublime_toml_highlighting

  DotENV      — authored for opencode (MIT). Minimal .env grammar.
  INI         — authored for opencode (MIT). Sections / key=value / comments.
  Dockerfile  — authored for opencode (MIT). Instructions / strings / vars.
  TypeScript  — authored for opencode (MIT). .ts/.tsx/.mts/.cts.
  Solidity    — authored for opencode (MIT). .sol.
  Move        — authored for opencode (MIT). .move (Aptos/Sui).
  Noir        — authored for opencode (MIT). .nr (Aztec).
  Circom      — authored for opencode (MIT). .circom (zkSNARK circuits).
  Cairo       — authored for opencode (MIT). .cairo (StarkNet).
  Vyper       — authored for opencode (MIT). .vy (EVM).
  Sway        — authored for opencode (MIT). .sw (Fuel).
  Cadence     — authored for opencode (MIT). .cdc (Flow).
  Leo         — authored for opencode (MIT). .leo (Aleo ZK).
  Yul         — authored for opencode (MIT). .yul (EVM IR).
  Huff        — authored for opencode (MIT). .huff (EVM assembly).
  ZoKrates    — authored for opencode (MIT). .zok (ZK DSL).
  Lean        — authored for opencode (MIT). .lean (theorem prover).

`.mjs`, `.cjs` and `.jsx` are aliased to the built-in JavaScript grammar.

Add your own: drop a `.sublime-syntax` file into
  ~/.config/opencode/syntaxes/   (or $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/opencode/syntaxes/)
and it is compiled into the syntax set at startup.
